A template offers a demand its first covering result whose binding is viable, not just
its first covering result: Pair[int, Name] near-misses Make through Pair[T, U] (int fails
Stringer) but pins cleanly through Pair[U, T], so Make serves it on the single-demand
step. That full pin preempts joint production. Rival, which could build Pair[int, Name]
only jointly, is not ambiguous with Make; it lifts V around the supplied demand while
serving Other[bool]. Stopping at the first covering result would leave the demand pending
and reject Make and Rival as ambiguous instead.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
output: plumb_gen.go
-- example.com/app/a.go --
package app

type Stringer interface{ String() string }
type Name string

func (n Name) String() string { return string(n) }

type Pair[A, B any] struct{}
type Other[W any] struct{}
type Sink struct{}

//plumb:build
func Make[T Stringer, U any]() (Pair[T, U], Pair[U, T]) { return Pair[T, U]{}, Pair[U, T]{} }

//plumb:build
func Rival[V, W any]() (Pair[int, V], Other[W]) { return Pair[int, V]{}, Other[W]{} }

//plumb:build
func NewSink(p Pair[int, Name], o Other[bool]) *Sink { return nil }
